WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.326
By default, Orchard Core keeps uploaded media in the App_Data folder.

00:00:05.326 --> 00:00:10.518
The Azure Media Storage feature keeps it in an Azure Blob Storage container instead.

00:00:10.518 --> 00:00:15.421
In this video, you configure the connection, enable the features, and follow an

00:00:15.421 --> 00:00:17.440
uploaded file to the browser.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.555
Containers and clean deployments can remove the files on the local disk of

00:00:26.555 --> 00:00:30.618
a web server, and several instances of a site need the same media.

00:00:30.618 --> 00:00:34.230
Azure Blob Storage keeps the files outside the web server.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.772
The Azure Media module has two features.

00:00:37.772 --> 00:00:42.506
Azure Media Storage keeps the media files in a blob container, and Azure

00:00:42.506 --> 00:00:46.209
Media Image Cache keeps resized copies of images there.

00:00:46.209 --> 00:00:50.700
A third entry, marked Obsolete, is kept for backwards compatibility.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.483
The site serves every file.

00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:58.536
A request reaches Orchard Core, which reads the blob from the container into

00:00:58.536 --> 00:01:02.294
a local cache folder when no copy exists, and sends it.

00:01:02.294 --> 00:01:05.560
Asset URLs do not point to the storage account.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.267
Azure Media Storage reads the OrchardCore, Media, Azure section of the configuration.

00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:20.532
Set a connection string and a container name that follows the Azure naming rules.

00:01:20.532 --> 00:01:24.950
If either is missing, the feature stays inactive and logs an error.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.628
Base Path stores the media in a subdirectory of the container.

00:01:29.628 --> 00:01:35.230
Create Container, true by default, creates the container at startup, without public access,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.790
if it does not exist.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.345
Keep the connection string out of source control.

00:01:40.345 --> 00:01:46.006
An environment variable overrides the application's configuration file, and two underscores separate each

00:01:46.006 --> 00:01:47.740
level of the setting's path.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.653
To try this without an Azure account, run the Azurite emulator in a

00:01:52.653 --> 00:01:58.242
container, using the image that the Azurite documentation names, and use development storage

00:01:58.242 --> 00:02:00.010
as the connection string.

00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.313
In the admin, open Tools, Features, and search for Azure Media.

00:02:08.313 --> 00:02:12.477
The features of the module are listed under the Hosting category.

00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:14.917
Enable Azure Media Storage.

00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:19.171
The Media Cache feature it depends on is enabled with it, and from

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:21.947
then on the media store is the container.

00:02:23.585 --> 00:02:27.602
Settings, Media, Azure Blob Options shows what the feature read.

00:02:27.602 --> 00:02:32.768
The connection string appears only as Configured, and the container name, base path,

00:02:32.768 --> 00:02:37.245
and check boxes are read-only, because they come from the configuration.

00:02:37.706 --> 00:02:41.758
Settings, Media, Options confirms the active store.

00:02:41.758 --> 00:02:48.441
Storage provider reads Azure Blob, Gen1, and Hierarchical namespace and Atomic move show

00:02:48.441 --> 00:02:52.636
crosses, so the store uses flat-namespace operations.

00:02:56.823 --> 00:02:58.312
Open Media, Library.

00:02:58.312 --> 00:03:02.836
The root folder is empty, because files that were already in the App_Data

00:03:02.836 --> 00:03:05.413
folder are not copied to the container.

00:03:05.864 --> 00:03:10.551
Choose Upload files, and select the picture that the home page uses as its banner.

00:03:10.551 --> 00:03:14.120
The site writes it to the container as a blob, with a content

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.304
type taken from the file extension.

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:17.919
Open the file.

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:22.028
Its address begins with the media path of your site, not with the

00:03:22.028 --> 00:03:24.169
address of the storage account.

00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:28.278
When a request finds no local copy, the site copies the blob into

00:03:28.278 --> 00:03:31.692
a cache folder that Settings, Media, Cache can purge.

00:03:35.892 --> 00:03:40.319
The feature switches the media services to the container only when the connection

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.355
string and container name are set.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:46.477
It replaces IMediaFileStore with a store over a Blob File Store, and serves

00:03:46.477 --> 00:03:49.072
files from a cache folder under the web root.

00:03:49.822 --> 00:03:55.012
When Media TUS Uploads is enabled, a second startup class replaces the temporary

00:03:55.012 --> 00:04:00.262
store for resumable uploads, so partial uploads are staged in the same container.

00:04:04.462 --> 00:04:09.232
Azure Media Image Cache has its own ImageCache section inside the Azure section,

00:04:09.232 --> 00:04:13.837
with its own connection string, container name, and base path, so the resized

00:04:13.837 --> 00:04:16.222
images can live in another container.

00:04:16.972 --> 00:04:20.449
It replaces IResizedImageCache with a blob-backed one.

00:04:20.449 --> 00:04:24.737
Resized images then persist when the local disk of the web server is

00:04:24.737 --> 00:04:28.272
replaced, and that disk is spared the reads and writes.

00:04:29.883 --> 00:04:33.348
Enable Azure Media Image Cache in Tools, Features.

00:04:33.348 --> 00:04:39.103
It depends only on the Media feature, so it can be enabled without Azure Media Storage.

00:04:39.594 --> 00:04:40.979
Open the home page.

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:45.302
Its banner is the uploaded picture, resized by the banner media profile.

00:04:45.302 --> 00:04:49.735
The first request resizes it and writes the result to the cache container,

00:04:49.735 --> 00:04:52.174
and later requests read it from there.

00:04:56.374 --> 00:04:59.175
Container Name and Base Path accept Liquid.

00:04:59.175 --> 00:05:03.235
Use the name of the tenant to give each tenant its own container,

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.866
or its own folder in a shared container.

00:05:05.866 --> 00:05:09.354
Only the default Liquid filters and tags are available.

00:05:10.104 --> 00:05:13.722
By default, removing a tenant leaves the container alone.

00:05:13.722 --> 00:05:18.489
Remove Container deletes it, and Remove Files From Base Path deletes only the

00:05:18.489 --> 00:05:22.567
files under the base path, and only when Remove Container is off.

00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:25.324
The image cache has Remove Container only.

00:05:26.074 --> 00:05:29.186
A Gen2 account has a hierarchical namespace.

00:05:29.186 --> 00:05:34.601
The store detects it at startup, and then uses real directories and atomic moves.

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:38.896
On a flat Gen1 account, a folder exists as a marker file inside

00:05:38.896 --> 00:05:40.764
it, which listings hide.

00:05:41.514 --> 00:05:45.039
Detection needs permissions on the storage account.

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:51.411
With a shared access signature scoped to one container, set UseHierarchicalNamespace yourself, to

00:05:51.411 --> 00:05:53.761
true for Gen2 or false for Gen1.

00:05:53.761 --> 00:05:57.164
The Media Library warns when the account is Gen2.

00:05:59.164 --> 00:06:01.175
That is Azure Media Storage:

00:06:01.175 --> 00:06:06.795
media files in a blob container, resized images in another, and settings for each tenant.

00:06:06.795 --> 00:06:08.334
Thanks for watching.

